<title>USA: U.S. Sept non-farm payrolls fell 40,000.</title>
<headline>U.S. Sept non-farm payrolls fell 40,000.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-10-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department</p>
<p>seasonally adjusted jobs data.</p>
<p> In 1,000s--Change     Sept    Aug--(Prev) July--(Prev) Sept95</p>
<p>  in Nonfarm Payrolls   -40    241    250    235   228     124</p>
<p> Jobless Rate (Pct)     5.2    5.1    5.1    5.4   5.4     5.6</p>
<p>Earnings, Hours of Private, Non-Farm Production workers:</p>
<p>    -			Sept   Aug--(Prev)  July-(Prev)  Sept95</p>
<p> Earnings/Hour(dlrs)   11.92  11.86  11.87  11.81  11.81  11.52</p>
<p> Avg Weekly Hours	34.7   34.5   34.4   34.2   34.3   34.4</p>
<p> Manufacturing Hours   41.8   41.7   41.7   41.6   41.6   41.5</p>
<p> -Overtime Hours	  4.5    4.5    4.4    4.4    4.4    4.4</p>
<p>Non-Farm Month-On-Month Payroll Changes by Industry:</p>
<p>    In 1,000s --	 Sept    Aug--(Prev) July--(Prev) Sept95</p>
<p> Goods-Producing	  -50     30     29    -10    -9	-3</p>
<p> -Construction		9     11	6     24    23	23</p>
<p> -Manufacturing	   -57     18     25    -29   -27     -24</p>
<p> Service-Producing	 10    211    221    245   237     127</p>
<p> -Retail		     22	4     21     97    88	33</p>
<p> -Services		   54     69     81     80    74     108</p>
<p> -Government		-81    109     77     31    39     -44</p>
<p>Aggregate Weekly Hours Indexes, Seasonally Adj. (1982=100)</p>
<p>    -		    September  August---(Prev)   Sept95</p>
<p> Total Private	    138.0    137.1    136.9    134.0</p>
<p> -Goods-Producing	 110.3    110.6    110.5    109.7</p>
<p> --Manufacturing	  106.1    106.3    106.2    106.6</p>
<p> -Service-Producing     150.5    149.0    148.8    144.9</p>
<p>Note--The indexes show total aggregate hours of production or nonsupervisory workers on private nonfarm payrolls by industry.</p>
<p>Number of Workers by Industry, Seasonally Adj. (1,000s):</p>
<p>    -		    September  August---(Prev)   Sept95</p>
<p> Total Non-Farm	 119,990  120,030  120,032  117,623</p>
<p> Goods-Producing	 24,245   24,295   24,295   24,176</p>
<p> -Construction	    5,447    5,438    5,432    5,187</p>
<p> -Manufacturing	  18,229   18,286   18,295   18,415</p>
<p> --Durable Goods	 10,673   10,707   10,714   10,648</p>
<p> --Non-Durable Goods    7,556    7,579    7,581    7,767</p>
<p>Number of Workers by Industry, Seasonally Adj. (1,000s):</p>
<p>    -		    September  August---(Prev)    Sept95</p>
<p> Service-Producing     95,745   95,735   95,737   93,447</p>
<p> -Transp./Utilities     6,343    6,340    6,348    6,194</p>
<p> -Wholesale Trade	 6,603    6,598    6,593    6,451</p>
<p> -Retail Trade	   21,698   21,676   21,684   21,258</p>
<p>-Finance, Insurance,</p>
<p>  and Real Estate	 7,005    6,998    7,007    6,842</p>
<p> --Services		34,586   34,532   34,538   33,377</p>
<p> --Help Supply	    2,394    2,387    2,393    2,216</p>
<p> -Government	     19,510   19,591   19,567   19,325</p>
<p>The non-farm payroll data are based on a survey of employers and the jobless rate is based on a survey of households.</p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected a September rise in non-farm payrolls of 166,000 and a jobless rate of 5.2 percent. They also had forecast a 0.2 percent rise in September average hourly earnings and an average work week of 34.5 hours.</p>
<p>The household survey also indicated that September employment rose 313,000, that the labor force expanded by 455,000, and the number of unemployed rose 141,000.</p>
